module

public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Limits.Constructions.Over.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.Quasicoherent.BigSiteColimits.AffineRefinement
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.Sheaves.FibreProducts

/-!
# Gluing the sheaf condition from a distinguished class of objects

A presheaf on a site in which a distinguished class of objects covers every object is a sheaf as
soon as it satisfies the sheaf condition for every covering sieve of every distinguished object
**and** for one covering family by distinguished objects of every other object.  The second
hypothesis cannot be dropped: `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.not_isSheaf_of_isSheafFor_objects_eqFalse`
exhibits a site with fibre products, a class of objects covering every object, and a presheaf
satisfying the sheaf condition for every covering sieve of every object of that class which is not
a sheaf.

This is the shape of Stacks 022H, which characterises the fpqc sheaves on the category of all
schemes as the Zariski sheaves satisfying the sheaf condition for a flat surjection of affines:
the condition at the affine objects and the condition for the Zariski coverings are two
independent inputs, and the second is about arbitrary — in particular non-affine — objects.  The
specialisation
`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.isSheaf_over_of_isSheafFor_affine_of_exists_isSheafFor` is the
corresponding statement for a slice of the associated fppf site of a category fibred in groupoids,
with the hypothesis at the non-affine objects isolated: the affine objects there cover every object
(`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.exists_affine_ofArrows_over`), so at an affine object the covering
family is supplied by the condition at affine objects itself, and only the non-affine objects
contribute a hypothesis.

## What the counterexample does and does not establish

A presheaf's sections over an object are pinned down only by a covering of that object for which
the sheaf condition is known.  A hypothesis quantified over the distinguished objects alone says
nothing at an object outside the class, which is what the counterexample makes precise: the site
is the two-element poset `false ≤ true` with the topology whose covering sieves are those
containing every morphism out of `false`, and the distinguished class is `{false}`, which covers
both objects.  The only covering sieve of `false` is the maximal one, so the condition at the
distinguished objects is vacuous, while the presheaf represented by `false` fails the sheaf
condition for the covering sieve of `true` generated by `false ⟶ true`.  The site is a finite
lattice, so it has fibre products (`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.hasPullbacks_bool`) and adding them to
the hypotheses does not rescue the implication.

The scope of that is exactly: the implication fails *for an arbitrary site*, so the hypothesis at
the objects outside the distinguished class cannot be dropped from
`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSheaf_of_isSheafFor_objects`, and no argument of that shape can supply
it.  It says nothing about any particular site.  In particular it leaves open whether the
corresponding statement holds on a slice of the associated fppf site of a category fibred in
groupoids, which carries far more structure than the two-element poset — affine open covers,
fibre products of schemes, faithfully flat descent — none of which the counterexample sees.  The
hypothesis `hnonaff` of
`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.isSheaf_over_of_isSheafFor_affine_of_exists_isSheafFor` is therefore
*open*, not refuted; what is refuted is deriving it from the condition at the affine objects by a
site-theoretic argument alone.

So the reduction below is the whole of the comparison step: the sheaf condition for the covering
families by affine objects of *every* object of a slice, which is what
`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.isSheaf_over_of_isSheafFor_affine_ofArrows` asks for, is reduced to
the sheaf condition for every covering sieve of every *affine* object together with one covering
family by affine objects per non-affine object.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSeparatedFor_of_isSheafFor_objects`: **a presheaf satisfying the two
  hypotheses is separated for every covering sieve of every object.**
* `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSheaf_of_isSheafFor_objects` and its form for a presheaf with values
  in an arbitrary category `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSheaf_of_isSheafFor_objects'`: **a presheaf
  satisfying the sheaf condition for every covering sieve of every distinguished object and for one
  covering family by distinguished objects of every object is a sheaf.**
* `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.not_isSheaf_of_isSheafFor_objects_eqFalse`: **the second hypothesis
  cannot be dropped**, even on a site with fibre products and for a distinguished class covering
  every object.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.isSheaf_over_of_isSheafFor_affine_of_exists_isSheafFor`: **a
  presheaf on a slice of the associated fppf site satisfying the sheaf condition for every covering
  sieve of every affine object, and for one covering family by affine objects of every non-affine
  object, is a sheaf.**

## Implementation notes

The amalgamation produced at an arbitrary object is glued from the amalgamations over the members
of the chosen covering family, and the verification that it restricts correctly along an arbitrary
arrow of the covering sieve uses the fibre products of that arrow with the members of the family;
this is the only place fibre products enter, and it is why the site is asked to have them.  In the
geometric application they are the fibre products of a category fibred in groupoids over the big
fppf category of schemes over `S`, Stacks 06WY.

Compatibility of the glued family and the separatedness step both compare two sections over an
object by restricting along the members of a covering family of it; the separatedness statement is
therefore proved first, for every covering sieve of every object, and used throughout the
construction of the amalgamation.

The index type of the covering families is a free universe parameter in
`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSheaf_of_isSheafFor_objects` and
`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSheaf_of_isSheafFor_objects'`, but *not* in the geometric
specialisation: there it is `Type (u + 1)` with `u` the universe of the schemes, the one in
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfSite.{u}`, because that is the universe of the index type of the affine
covering families `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.exists_affine_ofArrows_over` produces, and the
affine case of the proof consumes one of those.  A caller needing the hypothesis at another index
universe should use the site-level statement directly.

